{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 35, :comments ["<b>And he refused to be comforted.</b> For he thought he would be banished for his negligence, in that he sent him there." "<b>For I will go down to my son.</b> On account of my son — like <b>אל המקלות</b> (to the staves) which is <b>בשביל העון</b> (on account of the punishment): the suffering that has come upon me on account of my son, whom I sent to a place where I knew they hated him with a hatred of death — <b>ארד</b> (I will go down), but not Sheol." "<b>And his father wept for him.</b> This is Yitzhak. Rabbi Simon says: on account of all who mourn for him, they mourn with him. Rabbi Levi said: he would weep near him (אצלו), but as he went out from him he would eat and drink and bathe and anoint, as Rav said to Rabbi Hiyya his son: in a visible manner observe mourning; in an unobserved manner do not observe mourning. And so it is in Moed Katan, chapter אלו מגלחין."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:07:48.793484116Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}